WEBVTT


00:02:20.589 --> 00:02:21.578
Here you go, miss.


00:02:27.149 --> 00:02:28.218
Annie!


00:02:30.029 --> 00:02:31.382
Millman!


00:02:43.989 --> 00:02:45.263
I'll go.


00:02:45.349 --> 00:02:46.782
Hold on, Miss Kitty.


00:02:57.548 --> 00:03:00.267
-Hello, Evelyn. Come in.
-Kitty, darling.


00:03:01.628 --> 00:03:02.743
-Ready for the big day?
-Can't wait!


00:03:19.108 --> 00:03:22.145
-Well, good heavens, Mr Patten!
-Hello, Millman.


00:03:22.228 --> 00:03:25.823
Or is it Professor now?
We weren't expecting you today, sir.


00:03:25.908 --> 00:03:28.183
Well, Dolly asked me to come,


00:03:28.268 --> 00:03:30.259
and you know how hard it is to say no to her.


00:03:30.348 --> 00:03:32.737
We haven't seen you
since you went off to Greece.


00:03:32.828 --> 00:03:35.296
Or since Dolly went rushing off to Albania.


00:03:35.388 --> 00:03:37.265
Yes, a lot has happened.


00:03:38.428 --> 00:03:40.862
I have missed your custard.


00:03:42.628 --> 00:03:45.142
-That'd be the drawing room!
-Thank you, Mrs Whitstable.


00:03:45.228 --> 00:03:47.537
-Is Dolly down yet?
-Not yet, sir.


00:03:47.628 --> 00:03:50.301
-Would you like me to tell her you've arrived?
-Yes.


00:03:52.508 --> 00:03:54.179
No, no.


00:03:59.747 --> 00:04:02.625
-Annie!
-Really, Kitty, my poor head can't bear it.


00:04:02.707 --> 00:04:05.540
Don't you think you could go upstairs
and talk to them?


00:04:05.627 --> 00:04:08.983
Ooh! It's freezing. I'm more dead than alive.


00:04:09.067 --> 00:04:11.865
You are like a dear, elegant little fly.


00:04:11.947 --> 00:04:15.303
You must think me a kind of
great, clumsy rhinoceros in this frock.


00:04:15.387 --> 00:04:16.740
Don't be silly.


00:04:16.827 --> 00:04:21.139
No naval officer will show any interest
in a girl who looks like a giant cheesecake.


00:04:21.227 --> 00:04:24.424
Nonsense. Everybody will be looking
at the bride anyway.


00:04:33.267 --> 00:04:34.586
Millman?


00:04:35.227 --> 00:04:37.980
I wore my blue satin wedding shoes
down to dinner last night,


00:04:38.067 --> 00:04:40.979
and now the toes are all grey and dirty.


00:04:41.067 --> 00:04:43.183
Dreadful of me, I know,
and completely typical, I'm afraid.


00:04:43.267 --> 00:04:48.182
You ought not to have done that, miss.
We'll see what we can do with them.


00:04:48.267 --> 00:04:50.781
I do know one or two little tricks.


00:05:04.426 --> 00:05:05.939
Hello, Joseph.


00:05:06.026 --> 00:05:08.017
It's Professor Joseph to you, boy.


00:05:08.106 --> 00:05:09.175
What's that?


00:05:09.346 --> 00:05:10.381
Ah.


00:05:11.466 --> 00:05:14.185
They are homemade,
so the strength may vary.


00:05:17.226 --> 00:05:20.104
One every two hours,
preferably at mealtimes.


00:05:24.226 --> 00:05:29.141
-Is it true Joseph's coming?
-Dolly invited him. Lord knows if he'll come.


00:05:29.586 --> 00:05:32.623
-I predict a drama if he does.
-Oh, I do hope so.


00:05:34.106 --> 00:05:35.858
Where is Doll?


00:05:35.946 --> 00:05:38.096
Is the bride-to-be
moving slowly this morning?


00:05:38.186 --> 00:05:39.175
Like a snail.


00:05:46.466 --> 00:05:48.218
-Here.
-I don't want one.


00:05:48.306 --> 00:05:51.264
You're not going to enjoy
this afternoon without it. I can tell you.


00:05:51.346 --> 00:05:54.065
I'm not going to
enjoy this afternoon, full stop.


00:05:54.146 --> 00:05:55.135
Tom, hello.


00:05:55.266 --> 00:05:57.302
The women go crazy at weddings.


00:05:57.506 --> 00:06:00.065
They become totally uncontrollable.
Trust me, it's what they live for.


00:06:00.145 --> 00:06:02.022
Just watch Aunt Hettie.


00:06:03.505 --> 00:06:05.257
Good God, man,
what on Earth are those socks?


00:06:05.345 --> 00:06:08.178
-They are pure cat sick.
-Leave me alone, Tom.


00:06:08.265 --> 00:06:09.857
Robert, it is not at all fair


00:06:09.945 --> 00:06:12.743
to hold the family's reputation to ransom
by your vulgar choice of socks.


00:06:12.825 --> 00:06:15.385
I only wish this family had
a reputation worth protecting.


00:06:15.465 --> 00:06:17.456
My toes are like icicles.


00:06:17.545 --> 00:06:20.059
Trust me, Evie, this works like a charm.


00:06:20.145 --> 00:06:21.658
Golly, Tom, it's a bit early for that.


00:06:21.745 --> 00:06:24.054
This will put hairs on your chest.


00:06:24.145 --> 00:06:26.056
Here goes nothing.


00:06:27.425 --> 00:06:28.619
Ooh.


00:06:29.105 --> 00:06:30.777
What'll it be like
standing in that draughty church,


00:06:30.865 --> 00:06:32.537
holding a sopping wet bunch of flowers?


00:06:33.585 --> 00:06:36.145
One ofthese days you too will be married,
and then you won't talk like that.


00:06:36.225 --> 00:06:37.817
Not if I have anything to do with it.


00:06:37.905 --> 00:06:39.497
Evie, in spite of all the things you say,


00:06:39.585 --> 00:06:41.940
I think you'll make
the most wonderful mother.


00:06:42.905 --> 00:06:44.338
Some mothers eat their young.


00:06:54.105 --> 00:06:55.333
Oh!


00:06:55.425 --> 00:06:56.778
12:30 already.


00:06:56.865 --> 00:07:00.061
-Goodness!
-Hello, Evelyn, dear. Don't you look pretty?


00:07:00.144 --> 00:07:02.499
Mum, do you think I look
too exceptionally stupid in this getup?


00:07:02.584 --> 00:07:05.178
Hardly anyone here,
and the wedding at 2:00.


00:07:05.264 --> 00:07:07.619
Robert dear, can you reach
the top of the tree for the angel?


00:07:07.704 --> 00:07:09.296
You're just tall enough, I think.


00:07:09.384 --> 00:07:12.581
Kitty, I thought you were going to wear
your lovely glass brooch?


00:07:12.664 --> 00:07:14.939
Annie! I need that brooch!


00:07:15.024 --> 00:07:17.538
-Speak up.
-Here's the Prof!


00:07:18.544 --> 00:07:20.057
-Joseph.
-Evie.


00:07:20.664 --> 00:07:23.497
-Hello.
-Hello, Joseph.


00:07:23.584 --> 00:07:26.621
Dolly didn't mention
you'd be coming to luncheon.


00:07:26.704 --> 00:07:29.218
Then I must apologise
on behalf of your daughter, Mrs Thatcham.


00:07:29.304 --> 00:07:30.419
-She wrote personally...
-It's quite all right.


00:07:30.504 --> 00:07:33.302
It's Millman who'll be put out
to the change in arrangements.


00:07:33.384 --> 00:07:34.373
Oh.


00:07:34.464 --> 00:07:38.013
She's preparing a cold luncheon
in the nursery just for the family.


00:07:38.104 --> 00:07:40.777
What? It's ready in the dining room, Mum!


00:07:41.704 --> 00:07:42.693
Mum!


00:07:48.544 --> 00:07:51.012
I thought Dollywould be down by now.


00:07:51.984 --> 00:07:54.452
Dolly doesn't do anything
unless she wants to,


00:07:54.544 --> 00:07:58.298
no matter if half the county is kept waiting.
You know that.


00:08:00.984 --> 00:08:04.453
The tortoise unexpectedly put in
an appearance this morning.


00:08:05.623 --> 00:08:07.261
I'm surprised
she hasn't given it away.


00:08:07.343 --> 00:08:08.822
She would never do that.


00:08:10.583 --> 00:08:13.017
Dolly may not have come down yet,


00:08:13.423 --> 00:08:15.778
but you're the one
who doesn't seem to be here.


00:08:18.703 --> 00:08:21.092
You don't think something's wrong, do you?


00:08:21.583 --> 00:08:23.813
What could possibly go wrong?


00:08:24.343 --> 00:08:25.935
Mum!


00:08:29.743 --> 00:08:31.017
-I don't understand.
-Mum!


00:08:31.103 --> 00:08:32.934
No, Kitty, I particularly said the nursery.


00:08:33.023 --> 00:08:35.742
Never mind,
Mrs Whitstable can clear up after church.


00:08:35.823 --> 00:08:38.940
What? You haven't asked Mrs Whitstable?


00:09:05.142 --> 00:09:07.178
Who is it this time?


00:09:07.982 --> 00:09:09.654
Hello?


00:09:09.742 --> 00:09:11.812
Dolly? It's me.


00:09:13.902 --> 00:09:15.176
Are you coming down soon?


00:09:15.662 --> 00:09:17.175
Well, I'm not ready yet.


00:09:17.262 --> 00:09:19.014
Will you be finished soon?


00:09:19.102 --> 00:09:22.139
I'm afraid I just don't know.


00:09:22.542 --> 00:09:24.931
I really can't say.


00:09:25.022 --> 00:09:27.331
Well, I'll be downstairs.


00:09:37.502 --> 00:09:41.177
Come and meet my cousin, Miss Spoon.
This is Horace Spigott.


00:09:41.262 --> 00:09:42.297
Hmm.


00:09:42.382 --> 00:09:47.331
Miss Spoon is no longer a governess.
She's been in Africa on a mission.


00:09:47.422 --> 00:09:48.411
How do you do?


00:09:48.942 --> 00:09:50.295
He can't hear you, dear.
You have to speak up.


00:09:50.382 --> 00:09:52.850
-What?
-I said she's got to speak up!


00:09:52.942 --> 00:09:54.091
I told you it would be like this.


00:09:54.182 --> 00:09:56.138
-Don't start again.
-You've hardly stopped.


00:09:56.222 --> 00:09:57.450
There you two are.


00:09:57.542 --> 00:10:00.136
-You said to go to the nursery.
-Bob's up there now, I think.


00:10:00.222 --> 00:10:01.974
Well, we're all in the drawing room now.


00:10:02.062 --> 00:10:04.018
Has Jimmy come down?
He's slipped off again.


00:10:04.102 --> 00:10:05.978
So long as he's not setting fire
to the summer house again,


00:10:06.061 --> 00:10:07.779
I don't mind where he is.


00:10:08.981 --> 00:10:09.970
Well, we've been here 10 minutes,


00:10:10.061 --> 00:10:12.370
and already I want to strangle
a member of your family.


00:10:12.461 --> 00:10:14.691
I'm running right on schedule.


00:10:19.741 --> 00:10:23.973
-Is it chaos downstairs, Millman?
-No more than usual, miss.


00:10:24.061 --> 00:10:27.337
-These shoes have come up a treat.
-Good.


00:10:31.061 --> 00:10:33.370
-Mr Patten was asking...
-I know.


00:10:58.261 --> 00:10:59.819
I've come to say goodbye.


00:11:04.661 --> 00:11:05.650
Robert?


00:11:05.741 --> 00:11:09.016
Just smile at Hettie
once every half an hour. That's all I ask.


00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:11.056
Are you suddenly so concerned
about Hettie's feelings?


00:11:11.140 --> 00:11:12.493
I'm fond of Hettie.


00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:16.255
"Fond." The word we use
for those we cannot love.


00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:19.735
Hello.


00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:21.689
Remind me?


00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:23.771
That friend of Dolly's
we met last summer.


00:11:23.860 --> 00:11:25.851
Wasn't Dolly rather fond of him?


00:11:26.900 --> 00:11:28.618
I need a drink.


00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:31.055
You're fond enough of that.


00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:32.858
You drive me to it.


00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:35.249
Hello, Nancy.
Would you like one of these?


00:11:35.340 --> 00:11:37.058
Need a light, Prof?


00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:38.489
Oh.


00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:40.659
Thank you.


00:11:41.260 --> 00:11:44.457
-Nancy...
-Yes, I'm afraid so.


00:11:46.460 --> 00:11:50.089
-Why hasn't your sister come down yet?
-I don't know. Nobody knows.


00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:53.889
Oh! By the way,
how do we tell the Bigham twins apart?


00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:56.369
Tony has a limp, and Tiger has a moustache.


00:11:56.460 --> 00:11:59.770
Tony, limp. Tiger, 'tache.


00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:02.328
Kitty, go and get Uncle Bob.


00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:09.289
-Robert.
-Yes, Aunt Hettie.


00:12:09.380 --> 00:12:12.177
Go and sit down like a couple of good boys.


00:12:33.939 --> 00:12:35.975
-He's going for another!
-The bowler!


00:12:37.259 --> 00:12:38.851
Keep it tight, lads.


00:12:40.619 --> 00:12:42.530
Don't borrow time, lads.


00:12:46.819 --> 00:12:47.808
There was no other way
of getting there,


00:12:47.899 --> 00:12:50.254
so I actually walked the last bit
through the fields in the dark. No torch.


00:12:50.339 --> 00:12:51.328
Oh.


00:12:51.419 --> 00:12:53.410
It was like the blind leading the blind.


00:12:53.499 --> 00:12:55.774
The whole time, I kept thinking
some maniac has escaped from Broadmoor


00:12:55.859 --> 00:12:58.054
and was gonna leap out of a hedgerow
and strangle me.


00:12:58.139 --> 00:12:59.811
-Or worse.
-Dolly.


00:12:59.899 --> 00:13:02.015
Obviously, that didn't happen.


00:13:03.179 --> 00:13:04.498
Nothing ever does.


00:13:05.099 --> 00:13:06.134
Mmm.


00:13:07.019 --> 00:13:08.338
Joseph!


00:13:08.419 --> 00:13:09.772
Oh, Dolly!


00:13:10.859 --> 00:13:12.735
Dolly!


00:13:19.378 --> 00:13:21.892
-Hello.
-Hello.


00:13:28.258 --> 00:13:31.648
Would you like me to bring you
a drop of brandy, sir?


00:13:31.738 --> 00:13:33.410
No thanks, Millman.


00:13:34.618 --> 00:13:37.007
Do you know when Dolly's coming down?


00:13:37.098 --> 00:13:38.929
She was a little bit unwell this morning.


00:13:43.418 --> 00:13:46.057
Nice to have you back in Devon, sir.


00:13:58.578 --> 00:14:01.376
-Well, now where's Bob?
-Don't worry, Mum. He's just coming.


00:14:07.218 --> 00:14:10.733
Ah, Patten.
So you survived the Mediterranean?


00:14:10.818 --> 00:14:11.807
Just about, sir.


00:14:11.898 --> 00:14:15.207
That young man seems to be
walking backwards instead of forwards.


00:14:15.297 --> 00:14:17.731
I can't imagine how he ever gets anywhere.


00:14:18.537 --> 00:14:19.936
He's a friend of Dolly's.


00:14:20.017 --> 00:14:23.930
He was here all last summer.
Drove Millman mad, I expect.


00:14:24.017 --> 00:14:26.485
-Red or white, Horace?
-Red, please.


00:14:26.577 --> 00:14:28.647
-That came through loud and clear.
-What?


00:14:28.737 --> 00:14:32.446
Prof, what's the anthropologist's
interpretation of the English marriage ritual?


00:14:32.537 --> 00:14:33.526
Ignore him.


00:14:33.617 --> 00:14:36.006
I'd call this a ceremony, not a ritual.


00:14:36.097 --> 00:14:40.249
Pardon me, Mrs Thatcham,
but isn't Canon Dakin going to say grace?


00:14:40.937 --> 00:14:43.132
-Oh, my. Bob?
-Mmm-hmm.


00:15:18.896 --> 00:15:20.488
-Amen.
-Amen.


00:15:22.176 --> 00:15:26.374
Oh, yes, it's a ritual all right,
complete with live sacrifice.


00:15:26.456 --> 00:15:29.653
-Would that be Dolly or Owen?
-It's whoever says I do.


00:15:29.736 --> 00:15:32.250
Can someone remind me
which Bigham twin is which?


00:15:32.336 --> 00:15:34.611
Joseph says Tiger has a moustache.


00:15:34.696 --> 00:15:38.894
I was told once that Tony has a tattoo.
Although, it's never been located.


00:15:38.976 --> 00:15:41.092
Kitty, it is up to you to find it for us.


00:15:41.176 --> 00:15:43.053
Joseph, please don't be disgusting.


00:15:43.136 --> 00:15:44.489
Where is the bride?


00:15:45.216 --> 00:15:46.569
I do so want to see the dress.


00:15:46.656 --> 00:15:48.567
You know Dolly. She likes to take her time.


00:15:48.656 --> 00:15:50.328
She didn't take her time
about marrying Owen Bigham.


00:15:50.416 --> 00:15:53.931
Short engagements make for long marriages.
Just look at Nancy and David.


00:15:54.016 --> 00:15:55.654
Yes, just look at us.


00:15:55.736 --> 00:15:57.613
Or you and Roger, Hettie.
You didn't hang around.


00:15:59.136 --> 00:16:01.206
That was a very long time ago.


00:16:01.296 --> 00:16:02.570
Things were different then, of course.


00:16:02.656 --> 00:16:04.009
Maybe one of us
should take her something to eat.


00:16:04.096 --> 00:16:06.052
No, Joseph.
She doesn't want anything to eat.


00:16:06.136 --> 00:16:07.854
Her stomach is upset.


00:16:07.936 --> 00:16:09.733
-Is she all right?
-It's nerves.


00:16:09.936 --> 00:16:12.086
-Mmm?
-Nerves!


00:16:30.775 --> 00:16:33.687
So was the expedition to Greece
a great success, Joseph?


00:16:33.775 --> 00:16:37.085
Just the usual stuff from dead civilisations,
dust and skeletons.


00:16:37.175 --> 00:16:39.643
Ooh, skeletons? What happened to them all?


00:16:39.735 --> 00:16:42.329
How much do you know
about the mating habits of female snakes?


00:16:42.415 --> 00:16:44.007
Now, that's what I call a ritual.


00:16:44.095 --> 00:16:46.563
The female of the species
is more deadly than the male.


00:16:46.655 --> 00:16:49.374
Kitty, bring me that present
on the window seat.


00:16:49.455 --> 00:16:51.332
-I'd like to show everybody.
-Ugh.


00:16:52.535 --> 00:16:54.048
What is it?


00:16:54.135 --> 00:16:57.684
It is a wedding present
from Dodo Potts-Griffith


00:16:57.775 --> 00:16:59.731
just sent over by the chauffeur.


00:16:59.815 --> 00:17:03.285
She made the whole thing entirely herself,


00:17:03.375 --> 00:17:05.730
and it really is so very cheerful and pretty.


00:17:05.815 --> 00:17:08.283
-Yes, but what is it, Hettie?
-A lampshade, obviously.


00:17:08.375 --> 00:17:09.410
Dear God.


00:17:09.495 --> 00:17:12.532
I believe she learned how to do
this sort of thing from her own mother.


00:17:12.615 --> 00:17:14.970
Specially made for Dolly!


00:17:15.055 --> 00:17:16.408
Really? Why?


00:17:17.335 --> 00:17:18.324
Thank you.


00:17:20.574 --> 00:17:22.371
Mr Owen Bigham to see you, ma'am.


00:17:23.374 --> 00:17:26.013
Mrs Thatcham, this is too dreadful of me.


00:17:26.094 --> 00:17:30.087
I know this is the last place in the world
I ought to be at the present moment.


00:17:30.174 --> 00:17:33.211
The fact is, Dolly has got the ring.


00:17:34.094 --> 00:17:36.403
She took it to have it made smaller
at the jeweller's.


00:17:36.494 --> 00:17:39.770
She promised faithfully
she would give it back to Tiger


00:17:39.854 --> 00:17:42.243
but has apparently forgotten to do so.


00:17:42.334 --> 00:17:43.687
What a perfectly Dolly thing to do.


00:17:43.774 --> 00:17:46.208
Perhaps she gave it to Tony by mistake?


00:17:46.294 --> 00:17:48.888
Well, Robert, go upstairs
and ask her what she's done with it.


00:17:48.974 --> 00:17:50.248
-Me?
-Go on, Robert.


00:17:50.334 --> 00:17:52.165
Please, one of you
do something useful for once.


00:17:52.254 --> 00:17:54.643
-I'll go, Mrs Thatcham.
-Don't trouble yourself, Joseph.


00:17:54.734 --> 00:17:56.929
Robert appears more than capable.


00:18:02.014 --> 00:18:04.482
Oh, God. Bella.


00:18:09.014 --> 00:18:11.369
So the animals are being fed in here.


00:18:12.574 --> 00:18:16.203
Is there anything left
for little old me, I wonder?


00:18:16.294 --> 00:18:19.127
-Oh, breath like a dragon.
-Well, thank heavens for my chauffeur.


00:18:19.214 --> 00:18:22.489
I can never remember my way
through these tiny roads.


00:18:22.573 --> 00:18:25.292
Bella dear, this is Dolly's Owen.


00:18:25.493 --> 00:18:28.724
Ha! The groom is here before the wedding.
Priceless.


00:18:28.813 --> 00:18:29.802
How do you do?


00:18:29.893 --> 00:18:32.453
He's here to get the ring.
Dolly forgot to hand it over last night.


00:18:33.613 --> 00:18:36.844
Well, Hettie, there always
has to be a drama.


00:18:36.933 --> 00:18:39.811
Do sit down, Bella,
before you tire yourself out.


00:18:39.893 --> 00:18:43.681
I simply cannot tell you
how marvellous my chauffeur is.


00:18:44.373 --> 00:18:46.603
You know, he treats me like an empress.


00:18:46.693 --> 00:18:49.924
-He's almost as handsome as the car.
-Is he really?


00:18:50.013 --> 00:18:53.130
-Which route did you take, Bella?
-Bob. Hello, darling.


00:18:53.213 --> 00:18:55.010
I don't know,
but it was the one with the day trippers.


00:18:55.093 --> 00:18:56.082
Hmm.


00:18:56.173 --> 00:19:00.644
Where on Earth, I kept asking myself,
are they all going a week before Christmas?


00:19:00.733 --> 00:19:02.291
-Lapland, probably.
-What?


00:19:02.373 --> 00:19:03.931
Lapland!


00:19:04.613 --> 00:19:06.365
How do you put up with it, Hettie?


00:19:06.453 --> 00:19:08.569
How did your chauffeur
put up with it, I wonder?


00:19:08.653 --> 00:19:10.405
Just a little, David.


00:19:11.453 --> 00:19:13.364
A little more.


00:19:13.453 --> 00:19:14.932
Go on, man.


00:19:16.813 --> 00:19:18.929
-Thank you.
-While you're there, David.


00:19:19.013 --> 00:19:21.686
Tom, are you all ready for church?
The wedding is at 2:00.


00:19:21.773 --> 00:19:24.844
2:00! Oh, gosh.
It's not too late to call it off, Owen!


00:19:24.932 --> 00:19:26.365
Just don't tell Dolly that.


00:19:26.452 --> 00:19:27.601
He's high as a kite.


00:19:27.692 --> 00:19:29.205
Tom, why don't you go
for a walk in the garden?


00:19:29.292 --> 00:19:30.850
Puff adders, were they?


00:19:30.932 --> 00:19:33.765
Tom, take your father out
for some fresh air!


00:19:36.612 --> 00:19:39.001
For God's sake, Bob, "puffing adders"?


00:19:39.092 --> 00:19:40.127
Come on, Tom.


00:19:50.412 --> 00:19:53.131
-Hello, Joseph.
-Owen.


00:19:53.212 --> 00:19:54.884
-How are you?
-Wonderful.


00:19:54.972 --> 00:19:58.089
Been up since 6:00
and can't wait to get cracking.


00:19:58.772 --> 00:20:02.321
So how long will you be in Argentina?


00:20:02.412 --> 00:20:06.405
Two years to start with,
but after that, we can't be sure.


00:20:07.692 --> 00:20:10.843
You don't think she's lost the ring, do you?


00:20:10.932 --> 00:20:13.605
No, no. It'll turn up.


00:20:13.692 --> 00:20:14.966
-No.
-It's for your own good.


00:20:15.052 --> 00:20:16.929
People will think you're queer
or Italian, which is worse!


00:20:17.012 --> 00:20:18.001
Ow!


00:20:18.092 --> 00:20:19.286
Mind my vases!


00:20:19.372 --> 00:20:22.011
Sorry to interrupt.
Do you have the ring?


00:20:22.092 --> 00:20:24.811
She says it's in here somewhere,
but she's not sure where she put it.


00:20:24.892 --> 00:20:26.324
-Robert!
-Right.


00:20:26.411 --> 00:20:28.481
Everyone please remain calm


00:20:28.571 --> 00:20:30.482
and search the area nearest you.


00:20:30.571 --> 00:20:32.687
I'd search the servants if I were you.


00:20:32.771 --> 00:20:34.363
Good idea. Let's start with your chauffeur.


00:21:05.371 --> 00:21:07.282
I'm sure you can get her another ring.


00:21:07.371 --> 00:21:11.762
-What on Earth is going on?
-The groom is here. The ring is missing.


00:21:11.851 --> 00:21:14.319
-What's next?
-The bride will disappear.


00:21:14.411 --> 00:21:16.686
I wish someone would make me disappear.


00:21:16.771 --> 00:21:17.806
So do I.


00:21:18.291 --> 00:21:20.725
Are you ready to be important?


00:21:20.811 --> 00:21:22.847
Am I going to be important?


00:21:22.931 --> 00:21:25.491
Well, you've got to walk Dolly
down the aisle, haven't you?


00:21:25.571 --> 00:21:27.527
And what about you?


00:21:27.611 --> 00:21:29.043
Pure wallflower.


00:21:30.210 --> 00:21:32.360
Which is just the way I like it.


00:21:37.410 --> 00:21:42.530
Joseph, since you are here,
can you please join in like everyone else?


00:21:42.610 --> 00:21:44.328
I'm praying to St Anthony.


00:21:44.410 --> 00:21:47.925
Think I'll join you.
Patron saint of lost articles.


00:21:49.010 --> 00:21:50.159
And missing persons.


00:21:50.250 --> 00:21:52.081
Eureka! I've found it.


00:21:52.170 --> 00:21:53.888
Oh, thank heaven!


00:21:53.970 --> 00:21:56.245
Well done, Evie!


00:21:56.330 --> 00:21:57.524
St Anthony never lets me down.


00:21:57.610 --> 00:21:58.963
Come on, darling,
let's get you to the church.


00:21:59.050 --> 00:22:00.449
-Yes.
-All's well.


00:22:02.850 --> 00:22:05.000
Your prayers were answered.


00:22:05.090 --> 00:22:07.365
Well, there's a first time for everything.


00:22:10.450 --> 00:22:14.079
-You planning to bag the canon, Evie?
-Don't be ridiculous.


00:22:14.170 --> 00:22:16.809
-A widower with two obstreperous sons.
-A rich widower.


00:22:16.890 --> 00:22:19.120
It doesn't bear thinking about.


00:22:20.290 --> 00:22:22.565
Might be fun for a while, though.


00:22:22.650 --> 00:22:24.800
Is that what Dolly's thinking?


00:22:27.810 --> 00:22:31.279
-Why is she avoiding me?
-She's avoiding all of us, darling.


00:22:33.729 --> 00:22:35.959
I'm not sure why she asked me.


00:22:37.289 --> 00:22:39.086
And now that I'm here,
I don't know why I came.


00:22:39.169 --> 00:22:43.606
You came because you wanted to,
and because Dolly wanted you to be here.


00:22:43.689 --> 00:22:46.965
-She's got a funny way of showing it.
-So have you, Joseph.


00:22:47.049 --> 00:22:51.600
If you're just doing this to be difficult,
I'd get out while you still can.


00:22:51.689 --> 00:22:53.122
That's exactly what I want to tell Dolly.


00:22:53.209 --> 00:22:54.324
Whatever next?


00:22:54.409 --> 00:22:56.684
Evelyn, my dear, can you bring
Dolly's little bouquet to her?


00:22:56.769 --> 00:22:59.488
-It's somewhere in the kitchen.
-Of course.


00:23:01.969 --> 00:23:03.925
Time is ticking by, everyone.


00:23:04.009 --> 00:23:06.887
Very, very slowly.


00:23:07.489 --> 00:23:09.400
There you are, Horace!


00:23:09.489 --> 00:23:13.198
Oh, such a beautiful day for Dolly's wedding!


00:23:13.289 --> 00:23:14.881
Have I missed it?


00:23:14.969 --> 00:23:17.199
I've put you in the Chinese room,


00:23:17.289 --> 00:23:19.849
-and you have a very nice view from there.
-How lovely.


00:23:19.929 --> 00:23:20.964
The Chinese room?


00:23:21.049 --> 00:23:24.246
Mum, how many more people
are to go into the Chinese room?


00:23:28.649 --> 00:23:31.641
-Nancy, can I borrow you this afternoon?
-What is it, Hettie?


00:23:31.729 --> 00:23:34.481
Will you keep an eye open for Dolly
just in case I'm not around to watch her?


00:23:34.568 --> 00:23:37.446
-She's awfully tired.
-Certainly.


00:23:37.528 --> 00:23:40.998
That academic friend of hers...


00:23:41.088 --> 00:23:42.123
Mmm?


00:23:42.568 --> 00:23:44.206
-Joseph?
-Yes, him.


00:23:44.288 --> 00:23:46.199
I think he may try to be


00:23:47.688 --> 00:23:48.962
troublesome.


00:23:49.048 --> 00:23:50.083
Oh.


00:23:50.168 --> 00:23:54.320
-Unfinished business, you mean?
-Well, I don't know about that.


00:23:54.408 --> 00:23:56.478
Aren't they old enough
to look after themselves, Hettie?


00:23:56.888 --> 00:24:00.244
Yes, of course, Nancy dear,
but it would make me feel better all the same


00:24:00.328 --> 00:24:02.284
if you could, just...


00:24:02.368 --> 00:24:04.677
Yes, of course. I'll see what I can do.


00:24:04.768 --> 00:24:07.441
You and David are
such a lovely example to Dolly.


00:24:07.528 --> 00:24:10.725
-And we're both so very fond of you.
-That's true.


00:24:11.608 --> 00:24:14.600
Come along. We'll get your coat.


00:24:15.528 --> 00:24:17.439
Who is that girl?


00:24:17.528 --> 00:24:19.200
"Fond"?


00:24:20.208 --> 00:24:22.119
They say you can meet your future spouse
at a wedding,


00:24:22.208 --> 00:24:26.645
but that seems incredibly unlikely
unless I marry a half-wit or a relative.


00:24:26.728 --> 00:24:27.717
Or both.


00:24:27.808 --> 00:24:29.685
Do you think there'll be any men in uniform
at the reception?


00:24:29.768 --> 00:24:32.840
Look, Kitty, isn't Dolly dressed yet?


00:24:32.928 --> 00:24:35.521
If I were getting married,
I'd have been ready at dawn.


00:24:35.607 --> 00:24:37.962
If you were getting married,
it would be more like pistols at dawn.


00:24:38.047 --> 00:24:40.242
I suppose
that very narrow-minded people think


00:24:40.327 --> 00:24:43.637
that Dolly is the romantic sister,
and I'm the lumpy, sensible one.


00:24:43.727 --> 00:24:45.319
Nobody thinks you're sensible.


00:24:45.407 --> 00:24:48.365
Dolly is absolutely efficient
when it comes down to it.


00:24:48.447 --> 00:24:50.403
Impetuous, I'll grant you,


00:24:50.487 --> 00:24:53.206
but underneath it all,
she's 100% hard-boiled.


00:24:53.287 --> 00:24:56.438
Look, can't you get her to come down?
I need to...


00:24:56.527 --> 00:24:59.917
I'd like to wish her luck
whilst she's still an unmarried woman.


00:25:00.007 --> 00:25:01.759
Maybe it's Mum.


00:25:01.847 --> 00:25:04.042
She does make
living in this house unbearable.


00:25:04.127 --> 00:25:07.278
Look what she did
with Millman and luncheon.


00:25:07.367 --> 00:25:09.801
Sometimes I wonder if it's all an act.


00:25:09.887 --> 00:25:13.084
What could be a better disguise
than a fussy old widow?


00:25:13.167 --> 00:25:15.840
She is a curious person, really.


00:25:17.007 --> 00:25:18.918
Mum, I mean.


00:25:21.007 --> 00:25:23.202
Aren't you feeling well, Joseph?


00:25:23.687 --> 00:25:25.040
Oh, dear.


00:25:26.167 --> 00:25:28.920
I had counted on you as being
the one bright spot at this awful gathering.


00:25:31.087 --> 00:25:33.920
Whitstable?
Is that my tortoise come back again?


00:25:34.007 --> 00:25:35.804
Yes, miss.


00:25:35.887 --> 00:25:38.844
I've seen him snuggling up
against the glass-house stove pipe.


00:25:38.926 --> 00:25:40.279
He must think it's spring.


00:25:40.646 --> 00:25:43.080
Could you take her to the kitchen
and get the girls to pack her up for me?


00:25:43.166 --> 00:25:45.202
They can use a biscuit tin or something.


00:25:45.286 --> 00:25:47.277
I've just the thing for that, miss.
He'll be just fine.


00:25:47.366 --> 00:25:49.118
She'll be just fine, you mean?


00:25:51.846 --> 00:25:53.837
He's a she, Whitstable.


00:25:56.046 --> 00:25:58.799
-Is he really, miss?
-Yes, of course she is.


00:25:58.886 --> 00:26:03.357
I'm going to take her to South America.
It's not so cold and windy down there.


00:26:34.086 --> 00:26:35.439
Oh, my goodness.


00:26:38.446 --> 00:26:40.242
He's lovely.


00:26:41.685 --> 00:26:43.755
-Actually, he's a she.
-No.


00:26:43.845 --> 00:26:44.834
Mmm-hmm.


00:26:44.925 --> 00:26:47.120
She's lovely then.


00:26:47.205 --> 00:26:50.356
A bit masculine, but lovely.


00:26:51.685 --> 00:26:55.041
-Where did you get her?
-At a market in Cairo.


00:26:56.085 --> 00:26:58.883
-Have you been to Cairo?
-I have.


00:26:59.605 --> 00:27:01.277
But she's from a pet shop in Ealing.


00:27:04.605 --> 00:27:06.436
What shall we call her?


00:27:08.445 --> 00:27:10.481
I don't know.


00:27:10.565 --> 00:27:12.362
How about


00:27:14.165 --> 00:27:16.884
-Daphne?
-Daphne?


00:27:16.965 --> 00:27:20.719
That's a name for a girl
who is fair of face and fleet of foot.


00:27:20.805 --> 00:27:25.037
-This little one looks more like a Brunhilda.
-Brunhilda?


00:27:25.125 --> 00:27:29.118
You know, sometimes, Dolly,
you should call things as they really are.


00:27:31.525 --> 00:27:33.038
Dolly!


00:27:34.685 --> 00:27:35.879
Come on.


00:27:40.245 --> 00:27:42.804
-Did they find the ring?
-Evelyn saved the day.


00:27:42.884 --> 00:27:45.000
Joseph spotted it first.


00:27:45.084 --> 00:27:48.235
Anyway, good luck, old girl.


00:27:48.804 --> 00:27:50.396
Not that I need it.


00:27:51.684 --> 00:27:53.003
You certainly do not need it.


00:27:53.084 --> 00:27:56.156
Oh, how very, very awful of you, Dolly.


00:27:56.964 --> 00:27:59.159
You are ruining all the romance for me.


00:27:59.244 --> 00:28:02.361
A bride sitting upstairs
in her bedroom swigging rum!


00:28:02.444 --> 00:28:03.797
Out of a bottle.


00:28:03.884 --> 00:28:05.840
And in only a few minutes,
you'll be in church!


00:28:05.924 --> 00:28:09.394
-How could you?
-Well, you still have a lot to learn, my child.


00:28:09.484 --> 00:28:12.078
And why have you got my glass brooch?


00:28:12.164 --> 00:28:13.563
I've sent the whole household
looking for this.


00:28:13.644 --> 00:28:14.633
Oh, dear.


00:28:15.844 --> 00:28:17.163
I'm sorry to say it, Doll,


00:28:17.244 --> 00:28:19.519
but in some ways, it will be a good thing
when you're no longer here.


00:28:19.604 --> 00:28:21.003
Well, that's a nice thing to say to your sister


00:28:21.084 --> 00:28:24.121
on the eve of her departure
for the other side of the world.


00:28:24.204 --> 00:28:29.483
Doll, I know you are marvellously clever,
and interesting and witty.


00:28:29.564 --> 00:28:32.761
But I do think the way you look
at certain things is absolutely beastly!


00:28:32.844 --> 00:28:34.835
You know what I mean.


00:28:34.924 --> 00:28:35.913
Joseph was doing it earlier.


00:28:36.004 --> 00:28:38.518
He's downstairs saying
such stupid, awful things again.


00:28:38.604 --> 00:28:40.595
-What did he say?
-Oh, nothing, really.


00:28:40.684 --> 00:28:42.003
Kitty, what was he saying?


00:28:42.084 --> 00:28:43.277
Joseph is one of
those annoying guests


00:28:43.363 --> 00:28:47.117
upon whose good mood
the success of a party entirely depends.


00:28:47.203 --> 00:28:49.194
I don't know what
he's all so worked up about anyway.


00:28:49.283 --> 00:28:51.399
Oh, go on, Kitty,
tell us what Joseph was saying.


00:28:51.483 --> 00:28:52.962
Very well then.


00:28:53.603 --> 00:28:57.482
I told Joseph
that an Englishman in love lacks poetry.


00:28:57.563 --> 00:28:59.155
That was how it started.


00:28:59.243 --> 00:29:03.077
And they do. Englishmen do lack poetry.


00:29:03.163 --> 00:29:06.041
I told him about that awful Robinson boy
down at Malton. Do you remember?


00:29:06.123 --> 00:29:07.192
-No.
-You know,


00:29:07.283 --> 00:29:09.194
how when he was driving me home
after the dance that time,


00:29:09.283 --> 00:29:11.877
and his car packed up
at 5:00 in the morning.


00:29:11.963 --> 00:29:14.796
And instead of looking at the sunrise
or at me,


00:29:14.883 --> 00:29:18.398
all he could do was grumble along
with a face black as thunder.


00:29:18.483 --> 00:29:21.953
He kept saying,
"After tonight my name is mud in Malton!"


00:29:22.043 --> 00:29:23.078
"My dear chap," I said to him,


00:29:23.163 --> 00:29:26.712
"really, what does it matter
if your name is mud in a place like Malton?"


00:29:26.803 --> 00:29:30.637
I told Joseph I envied Barbara Mackenzie
her Spanish naval officer.


00:29:30.723 --> 00:29:32.679
Who wouldn't?


00:29:32.763 --> 00:29:35.277
He plays to her on his ukulele
in the moonlight,


00:29:35.363 --> 00:29:37.752
and he's not ashamed of his love for her.


00:29:37.843 --> 00:29:40.311
Oh! I'm going to Spain next year,
by the way, Doll, if mum will let me.


00:29:40.403 --> 00:29:44.237
-She won't let you go.
-Why not? She let you go to Albania.


00:29:44.642 --> 00:29:47.714
Anyway, I told Joseph I thought
he'd play the ukulele beautifully,


00:29:47.802 --> 00:29:50.635
and I could not imagine him ever being
ashamed of his love for a woman.


00:29:50.722 --> 00:29:51.950
I'm sure he loved hearing that.


00:29:52.042 --> 00:29:55.671
Well, I guess not, because after that,
he became quite ratty for some reason,


00:29:55.762 --> 00:29:58.481
and he started telling me
in that lecturer's voice of his...


00:29:58.562 --> 00:30:01.838
You must know, Kitty, that I don't at all care
for this snobbish continental talk of yours


00:30:01.922 --> 00:30:03.435
about foreigners, love, poetry, and...


00:30:03.522 --> 00:30:06.161
-Ukuleles.
-Sounds like he rehearsed that speech.


00:30:06.242 --> 00:30:09.837
Wait till you hear the rest. He said,


00:30:09.922 --> 00:30:13.073
"You must understand, Kitty, that there are
still some of us left who do not


00:30:13.162 --> 00:30:16.074
-"appreciate that attitude in our womenfolk. "
-Appreciate that attitude in our womenfolk.


00:30:16.162 --> 00:30:19.279
-He actually used the word "womenfolk"!
-Of course he did.


00:30:19.362 --> 00:30:21.239
Then he said, "My own aim is still


00:30:21.322 --> 00:30:24.041
"the clean-limbed, dirty-minded,
thorough English gentleman."


00:30:24.122 --> 00:30:25.680
And I still have hopes of being one.


00:30:25.762 --> 00:30:27.514
He's halfway there already.


00:30:27.602 --> 00:30:30.275
An English gentleman is not dirty-minded!


00:30:30.362 --> 00:30:31.431
How do you know he isn't?


00:30:31.522 --> 00:30:34.753
How do I know?
Is Uncle Bob dirty-minded?


00:30:35.522 --> 00:30:37.080
Was Dad dirty-minded?


00:30:37.162 --> 00:30:39.073
Oh, terribly, terribly.


00:30:39.162 --> 00:30:40.311
You're drunk!


00:30:40.402 --> 00:30:42.962
Well, I think it's perfectly disgusting!


00:30:49.441 --> 00:30:51.909
I don't mind telling you, Evie,
but we've been treated to these comments


00:30:52.001 --> 00:30:55.073
from Kitty every single day
for the last 12 months.


00:30:56.481 --> 00:31:00.394
English gentlemen
and Spanish guitar players.


00:31:00.481 --> 00:31:02.551
The whole house rocks with it.


00:31:03.561 --> 00:31:06.553
Joseph likes to stir up the wasps' nest
a little with a stick


00:31:06.641 --> 00:31:07.756
and then run away and hide.


00:31:11.361 --> 00:31:12.953
Then of course,


00:31:14.001 --> 00:31:17.596
the wasps fly straight upstairs
and sting innocent people.


00:31:19.001 --> 00:31:20.798
Dolly, what's wrong?


00:31:26.561 --> 00:31:30.349
Do you ever feel as though
you're just reading about yourself,


00:31:31.201 --> 00:31:35.558
Iike it's all in a book from the lending library
to be returned when you've finished?


00:31:37.121 --> 00:31:41.000
All that time last summer,
we were practically inseparable.


00:31:43.041 --> 00:31:46.511
-What if...
-What if what, Doll?


00:31:46.601 --> 00:31:49.353
What if he begged me to run off with him
by the back door, across the fields


00:31:49.440 --> 00:31:52.716
while everyone was sitting in the church
waiting for me?


00:31:52.800 --> 00:31:54.518
For goodness sake, what would I do?


00:31:54.600 --> 00:31:57.512
Well, for goodness sake,
what would you do?


00:31:59.480 --> 00:32:02.153
You do want to marry Owen, don't you?


00:32:02.960 --> 00:32:04.473
Of course I do.


00:32:05.800 --> 00:32:07.199
It's just...


00:32:13.120 --> 00:32:15.190
All is well.


00:32:15.280 --> 00:32:18.795
He does not care for me,
and therefore will not miss me.


00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:21.274
La di da.


00:32:22.960 --> 00:32:24.712
Cheer up, darling.


00:32:24.800 --> 00:32:29.430
Soon you will be bathing under blue skies
and resting in the shade of palm trees.


00:32:30.400 --> 00:32:32.789
I'm just talking nonsense, aren't I?


00:32:34.920 --> 00:32:37.434
You will faithfully promise to come out
and stay with us, won't you?


00:32:37.520 --> 00:32:39.636
-Of course.
-Owen insists on paying for your passage,


00:32:39.720 --> 00:32:41.836
and he can afford it, I assure you.


00:32:41.920 --> 00:32:43.956
I couldn't possibly exist there
for long without you.


00:32:44.040 --> 00:32:46.031
And Owen says he couldn't either.


00:32:47.040 --> 00:32:49.349
I wouldn't miss it for the world.


00:32:51.519 --> 00:32:53.953
He is lovely, your husband-to-be.


00:32:57.759 --> 00:32:58.748
I know.


00:33:03.159 --> 00:33:06.151
Perhaps when you come,
you'll bring a husband with you.


00:33:07.199 --> 00:33:08.473
Whose?


00:33:11.519 --> 00:33:14.909
Ready, darling?
Evelyn dear, you had better go downstairs.


00:33:19.679 --> 00:33:21.271
Courage, cherié.


00:33:32.039 --> 00:33:34.553
Your father would be so proud of you.


00:33:43.919 --> 00:33:45.750
And so am I.


00:33:54.558 --> 00:33:56.469
Do go down, Mother.


00:33:57.518 --> 00:33:59.076
I can do that.


00:34:00.958 --> 00:34:03.836
I have one or two last things to finish off.


00:34:18.198 --> 00:34:21.315
In spite of this heat, summer is over.
I can tell.


00:34:22.878 --> 00:34:24.675
Is Kitty back yet?


00:34:26.718 --> 00:34:28.310
Not yet.


00:34:28.398 --> 00:34:30.787
You'll get a crick in your neck
if you sit like that.


00:34:30.878 --> 00:34:32.470
-I'm fine.
-Hmm.


00:34:33.198 --> 00:34:34.870
What are you reading?


00:34:48.038 --> 00:34:52.634
"lf only I could go with him
to the edge of a precipice and say,


00:34:52.718 --> 00:34:54.515
"One more step, and I shall be over,


00:34:54.598 --> 00:34:57.987
"and then pale with fear,
he would catch me in his arms


00:34:58.077 --> 00:35:00.750
"and hold me over the edge
until my blood froze,


00:35:00.837 --> 00:35:03.431
"and carry me off wither he pleased."


00:35:09.677 --> 00:35:12.635
-You gave me this book.
-Did l?


00:35:13.797 --> 00:35:15.310
It was your father's, I think.


00:35:15.397 --> 00:35:19.276
Is it about adventure?
That was what he looked for in a book.


00:35:24.797 --> 00:35:26.469
Joseph was reading it.


00:35:27.157 --> 00:35:30.593
-Isn't he leaving any day now?
-I believe so.


00:35:30.677 --> 00:35:34.352
I hear Owen Bigham is off to Europe
with the Diplomatic Service.


00:35:44.077 --> 00:35:46.147
You should have an adventure.


00:35:46.237 --> 00:35:49.195
You've talked about travel so often.
I think you should do it.


00:35:49.277 --> 00:35:52.952
How about ltaly or Albania?


00:35:54.517 --> 00:35:55.950
Oh, heavens.


00:35:59.236 --> 00:36:01.625
The washing's only just been hung.


00:36:03.436 --> 00:36:06.553
-I'll think about it.
-That thunder sounds close.


00:36:15.196 --> 00:36:17.107
I've come to say goodbye.


00:36:26.556 --> 00:36:29.468
-Where on Earth's Tom?
-No idea, Dad.


00:36:29.556 --> 00:36:30.545
Oh.


00:36:30.636 --> 00:36:33.150
-Tell the vicar we're on our way.
-Yeah.


00:36:38.556 --> 00:36:40.672
The door jammed!


00:36:40.756 --> 00:36:42.553
Didn't anyone hear my knocking?


00:36:42.636 --> 00:36:44.592
Of course. We all did.


00:36:46.956 --> 00:36:49.026
Dolly, only five more minutes.


00:36:49.116 --> 00:36:51.072
All going smoothly, I hope.


00:36:51.556 --> 00:36:53.353
Are you ready, my dear?


00:37:02.755 --> 00:37:04.586
For God's sake, Dolly.


00:37:18.995 --> 00:37:20.144
-Oh!
-Oh!


00:37:28.475 --> 00:37:29.828
Let us dance.


00:37:53.435 --> 00:37:54.424
Whoo!


00:38:32.994 --> 00:38:34.552
Stop! You stood on my foot.


00:38:56.754 --> 00:38:59.871
Tom. Tom, you can hear me.
You really hurt my foot.


00:39:00.874 --> 00:39:01.989
Tom.


00:39:06.993 --> 00:39:10.952
-Always best to steer clear of the cider.
-That stuff'd kill a horse.


00:39:11.513 --> 00:39:12.832
Two ales, please.


00:39:14.113 --> 00:39:15.751
Two ciders, please.


00:39:21.673 --> 00:39:23.903
I could swear my toe is broken.


00:39:23.993 --> 00:39:26.348
The way you go on,
one would think dancing was all about feet.


00:39:26.433 --> 00:39:28.151
Kitty, I'm not sure
you had the perfect partner.


00:39:28.233 --> 00:39:30.747
I know. I've got the bruises to prove it.


00:39:30.833 --> 00:39:33.870
Waltzing time, ladies and gentlemen.
Waltzing time.


00:39:41.913 --> 00:39:42.902
Come on.


00:39:42.993 --> 00:39:44.824
-No, no, no...
-Come on, come on!


00:39:45.513 --> 00:39:46.707
Oh, God.


00:40:01.953 --> 00:40:03.511
-Come on.
-Really?


00:40:11.032 --> 00:40:12.385
-Have you waltzed before?
-No.


00:40:12.472 --> 00:40:14.542
Okay, do that with your right foot.


00:40:14.632 --> 00:40:16.429
-All right.
-Are you ready?


00:40:20.472 --> 00:40:23.828
-Very good. You're a natural.
-Am I doing well?


00:40:28.392 --> 00:40:30.144
Very good at that.


00:41:21.111 --> 00:41:23.067
What was all that about?


00:42:19.510 --> 00:42:20.625
Dolly.


00:42:22.470 --> 00:42:23.869
Dolly?


00:42:30.870 --> 00:42:33.259
The car's waiting for us, my dear.


00:42:35.830 --> 00:42:38.185
Ah. There you are, miss.


00:42:39.310 --> 00:42:41.983
Excuse me, miss, could you spare time
to step into the kitchen


00:42:42.070 --> 00:42:43.947
and let my old mother see you
afore the ceremony?


00:42:46.110 --> 00:42:47.702
Of course I will.


00:42:52.470 --> 00:42:55.701
She's got her heart set on seeing you
on your wedding day.


00:42:56.950 --> 00:42:57.985
Dolly?


00:43:00.150 --> 00:43:01.265
Betty.


00:43:02.630 --> 00:43:04.302
Hello, Mrs Whitstable.


00:43:05.150 --> 00:43:07.664
Don't get up. Please, please.


00:43:07.750 --> 00:43:10.423
I remember you when
you come back to me


00:43:10.510 --> 00:43:14.866
bringing your little doggie in your arms
after that motor car accident.


00:43:15.789 --> 00:43:17.381
That was Kitty, Mrs Whitstable.


00:43:17.749 --> 00:43:19.944
Her memory ain't quite what it used to be.


00:43:21.669 --> 00:43:25.218
And now you have growed up
into a fine, handsome woman,


00:43:25.309 --> 00:43:29.018
and your husband will be
a fine, handsome man.


00:43:29.109 --> 00:43:33.466
And you be proud of him,
and he'll be proud of you,


00:43:33.549 --> 00:43:35.858
and you be proud of one another.


00:43:36.589 --> 00:43:37.783
Dolly, I...


00:43:39.789 --> 00:43:44.226
You look very beautiful
in your wedding dress. Like a picture.


00:43:44.309 --> 00:43:45.901
Very pretty indeed.


00:43:47.829 --> 00:43:50.059
Of course, I lost my eyesight.


00:43:50.829 --> 00:43:53.662
You must have a piece ofwedding cake
later on, Mrs Whitstable.


00:43:53.749 --> 00:43:56.217
Oh, I couldn't fancy nothing to eat now, dear.


00:43:56.309 --> 00:43:58.777
-All right, Mother.
-Yes.


00:43:58.869 --> 00:44:02.464
You looked so very happy last summer.


00:44:03.429 --> 00:44:06.148
I knew it, I did.


00:44:07.429 --> 00:44:09.147
Whatever you do,


00:44:09.229 --> 00:44:15.098
just enjoy yourself
as hard and sure as ever you can.


00:44:15.188 --> 00:44:18.385
And you'd better do it
while you're young and pretty.


00:44:20.108 --> 00:44:22.542
That's what they call happiness, my girl.


00:44:25.708 --> 00:44:30.543
I always thought happiness was
something quite different, Mrs Whitstable.


00:44:30.628 --> 00:44:33.381
But whichever of us is right, thank you.


00:44:35.068 --> 00:44:36.660
Thank you ever so much.


00:44:36.748 --> 00:44:39.820
Your car is waiting for you outside,
Miss Dolly.


00:44:43.788 --> 00:44:45.904
Goodbye, Mrs Whitstable.


00:45:17.427 --> 00:45:18.416
Oh!


00:45:18.507 --> 00:45:19.781
Christ!


00:45:22.707 --> 00:45:24.937
-Oh, God.
-Oh, God.


00:45:27.467 --> 00:45:30.300
What am I going to do?
I can't go into church with a rum stain.


00:45:30.387 --> 00:45:33.504
-Oh, for God's sake. Listen, Dolly...
-Help me.


00:45:33.587 --> 00:45:35.896
Suggest something. Suggest something.


00:45:35.987 --> 00:45:37.625
-Dolly...
-Please, Joseph,


00:45:37.707 --> 00:45:40.096
run upstairs and get mother's scarf
out of the bottom drawer.


00:45:40.187 --> 00:45:42.621
The chest in my dressing room.


00:45:42.707 --> 00:45:44.345
-Please!
-Dolly, dearest Dolly...


00:45:44.427 --> 00:45:47.419
Quickly, Joseph.
A white lace scarf. Quickly!


00:46:01.267 --> 00:46:02.985
-Dolly?
-Coming.


00:46:03.067 --> 00:46:04.705
Put it through there.


00:46:06.147 --> 00:46:07.375
For God's sake, tell me one thing.


00:46:07.467 --> 00:46:09.662
I'll tell you anything in the world you like,
but afterwards.


00:46:09.747 --> 00:46:11.624
Here, just here.


00:46:12.947 --> 00:46:14.460
-All right.
-Against the stain.


00:46:14.547 --> 00:46:16.378
-In front of the stain.
-I'm doing it.


00:46:16.467 --> 00:46:18.343
For God's sake, hurry!


00:46:18.426 --> 00:46:20.178
What are you doing drinking rum anyway?


00:46:20.266 --> 00:46:23.383
You of all people should know a little
fortifying rum never did anyone any harm.


00:46:23.466 --> 00:46:26.139
I don't remember it
being part of traditional bridal preparation.


00:46:26.226 --> 00:46:29.616
What are you doing galloping around
the house frightening brides?


00:46:33.746 --> 00:46:36.465
Dolly, we've really got to go now!


00:46:37.146 --> 00:46:38.420
Dolly...


00:46:40.706 --> 00:46:43.095
Time and tide wait for no man.


00:47:07.906 --> 00:47:09.624
Joseph.


00:47:09.706 --> 00:47:11.901
Joseph, it's your go again.


00:47:11.986 --> 00:47:13.419
Right-o.


00:47:19.945 --> 00:47:21.173
Ha-ha!


00:47:21.425 --> 00:47:22.824
Great.


00:47:22.905 --> 00:47:25.260
Destroy your cousin for me, will you?


00:47:26.745 --> 00:47:29.305
I thought we could row down the river
and see the others.


00:47:29.385 --> 00:47:31.580
Millman has made us
the most delicious picnic.


00:47:31.665 --> 00:47:33.417
-What a lovely idea.
-Hmm.


00:47:38.025 --> 00:47:39.856
Where are you off to, my dear?


00:47:39.945 --> 00:47:42.140
Joseph wants to row me
down the river to see the others.


00:47:42.225 --> 00:47:43.419
Oh, really?


00:47:44.865 --> 00:47:46.662
I guess you could help me
dead-head the roses later.


00:47:46.745 --> 00:47:49.623
Don't tell me you're leaving me here
with this lot?


00:47:49.705 --> 00:47:52.173
Thank you for the tea, Mrs Thatcham.


00:47:59.865 --> 00:48:01.696
Let's go!


00:48:01.785 --> 00:48:02.934
Let's go!


00:48:03.825 --> 00:48:05.019
Catching up!


00:48:05.105 --> 00:48:06.697
No, you're not!


00:48:08.905 --> 00:48:10.179
Go on, Dolly.


00:48:14.105 --> 00:48:15.663
-I'll never...
-Whoo-hoo!


00:48:15.745 --> 00:48:16.814
Whoo!


00:48:17.065 --> 00:48:18.896
Whoo!


00:48:38.904 --> 00:48:40.098
-Are you ready?
-Yes.


00:48:40.184 --> 00:48:42.300
-Steady?
-Let's go.


00:49:26.063 --> 00:49:28.896
Remember that strange man...
The one with the moustache?


00:49:29.383 --> 00:49:30.816
-I guess it was.
-Very hot.


00:49:36.663 --> 00:49:38.938
-Good day.
-Hello.


00:49:46.423 --> 00:49:48.095
Welcome to the picnic.


00:49:48.983 --> 00:49:50.257
Hello.


00:49:52.423 --> 00:49:54.095
Evie, hello.


00:49:57.023 --> 00:49:59.742
-That's how it is.
-I absolutely love cooking.


00:50:04.583 --> 00:50:06.460
-They're lovely.
-Pass me a bun.


00:50:06.543 --> 00:50:08.261
Come on.


00:50:08.343 --> 00:50:10.811
This is one of those days
in the middle of winter,


00:50:10.903 --> 00:50:13.258
it is impossible to believe can exist.


00:50:13.903 --> 00:50:16.815
I'm not at all sure it exists even now.


00:50:16.903 --> 00:50:18.814
Isn't it beautiful?


00:50:19.783 --> 00:50:21.455
Yes. Very beautiful.


00:50:22.463 --> 00:50:23.452
Yes, please.


00:50:24.183 --> 00:50:25.774
Oh!


00:50:26.902 --> 00:50:31.532
Do you feel less happy
if you know you are happy?


00:50:32.902 --> 00:50:36.212
Usually, by the time you know,
it's already too late.


00:50:37.382 --> 00:50:38.531
Hmm.


00:50:40.742 --> 00:50:43.415
So when do you know
if you will be going on the expedition?


00:50:43.502 --> 00:50:44.617
August.


00:50:45.942 --> 00:50:47.660
You should come with me.


00:50:47.742 --> 00:50:49.061
I mean it.


00:50:49.142 --> 00:50:51.372
And I meant it when I laughed.


00:50:58.142 --> 00:51:02.215
I wish you'd show me one good reason
why I should come with you.


00:51:02.302 --> 00:51:04.452
You should be careful what you wish for.


00:51:04.542 --> 00:51:06.498
You sound like my mother.


00:51:15.822 --> 00:51:16.811
Oh.


00:51:21.262 --> 00:51:23.856
Imagine how long it took to die.


00:51:27.701 --> 00:51:29.976
We should get back to the others.


00:51:34.741 --> 00:51:37.096
Why are you in such
a hurry all of a sudden?


00:51:37.181 --> 00:51:39.092
Time and tide wait for no man.


00:51:42.301 --> 00:51:44.861
There you go. All right.


00:51:46.221 --> 00:51:47.893
We're all set.


00:51:47.981 --> 00:51:49.539
You're on!


00:51:50.061 --> 00:51:51.289
Dolly.


00:51:55.381 --> 00:51:59.090
What was it you were saying
about time and tide?


00:52:00.061 --> 00:52:01.653
You won't go far.


00:52:02.461 --> 00:52:05.419
Well, what are you waiting for?


00:52:05.501 --> 00:52:07.378
-Dolly!
-Damn!


00:52:07.461 --> 00:52:09.770
-Doll, what are you up to now?
-Ugh!


00:52:13.181 --> 00:52:14.933
We're turning around.


00:52:20.981 --> 00:52:23.370
-Joseph!
-Man overboard!


00:52:23.461 --> 00:52:24.780
-Joseph, I can't believe it.
-Turn around.


00:52:24.861 --> 00:52:26.055
Launch the life boats.


00:52:26.141 --> 00:52:28.450
-Joseph!
-Dolly, are you all right?


00:52:28.541 --> 00:52:30.292
You want a towel?


00:52:39.660 --> 00:52:42.333
Why did you shove off like that?


00:52:42.420 --> 00:52:44.456
What did you want me to do?


00:52:45.820 --> 00:52:47.936
I wanted you to come after me.


00:52:49.980 --> 00:52:52.130
Then you got what you wanted.


00:52:52.820 --> 00:52:55.050
Now, you want something else.


00:53:21.540 --> 00:53:23.690
Someone's been at the rum, then?


00:53:27.740 --> 00:53:30.732
Funny time to be drinking rum.


00:53:30.820 --> 00:53:32.571
On a wedding morning.


00:53:34.299 --> 00:53:36.051
I think Dolly spilt some.


00:53:36.859 --> 00:53:40.693
Like I say, funny time to be drinking rum.


00:53:42.619 --> 00:53:44.769
Don't fancy weddings then?


00:53:45.619 --> 00:53:47.177
Not this one, no.


00:53:47.259 --> 00:53:51.616
Well, we're not all made for it, you know.


00:53:54.059 --> 00:53:58.735
My husband said so every day of his life.


00:54:00.699 --> 00:54:02.690
And he should know.


00:54:05.859 --> 00:54:08.931
Don't seem natural,
this house being so quiet.


00:54:10.899 --> 00:54:12.969
Never was a peaceful house.


00:54:13.939 --> 00:54:15.133
Quite.


00:54:16.819 --> 00:54:20.129
Oh, thank you, Mrs Whitstable. You are kind.


00:54:20.219 --> 00:54:21.652
Can I help you to the kitchen?


00:54:21.739 --> 00:54:24.617
Oh, don't you fuss yourself, Mrs M.


00:54:24.699 --> 00:54:27.577
It's been a pleasure
talking to this young man.


00:54:30.019 --> 00:54:34.489
I'll go and see to them partridges,
and then I'll be off home and out your way.


00:54:36.698 --> 00:54:40.327
-You ought to be at the service, sir.
-Yes, Millman, I know,


00:54:42.178 --> 00:54:44.248
but circumstances intervened.


00:54:45.938 --> 00:54:50.011
Tell me, Millman,
why do you think people get married?


00:54:51.458 --> 00:54:55.292
Sir, when I first came here,
Miss Dolly was only seven years old.


00:54:56.418 --> 00:54:58.932
Now, she's a young woman with a husband


00:54:59.018 --> 00:55:02.169
and soon, God willing, a family of her own.


00:55:04.258 --> 00:55:09.286
People get married, because, as you say,
circumstances intervene.


00:55:10.538 --> 00:55:13.211
We rarely see them coming, though, do we?


00:55:15.218 --> 00:55:17.254
And that's life, isn't it?


00:55:19.458 --> 00:55:22.734
If Mrs Thatcham hadn't sent
Miss Dolly to Albania,


00:55:23.818 --> 00:55:26.412
none of this might ever have happened.


00:55:27.618 --> 00:55:29.848
Circumstances, you see.


00:55:35.777 --> 00:55:38.371
Do you think you could telephone
for a taxi to take me to the station, please?


00:55:38.457 --> 00:55:40.175
Of course, sir.


00:55:40.257 --> 00:55:43.215
Might be a while before it comes, though.
You know what they're like.


00:55:43.297 --> 00:55:44.969
That's fine.


00:56:26.817 --> 00:56:28.569
I'm leaving tomorrow.


00:57:55.055 --> 00:57:57.011
She's blind, isn't she?


00:58:03.815 --> 00:58:05.726
She sees everything.


00:58:12.655 --> 00:58:14.168
Congratulations.


00:58:16.015 --> 00:58:17.812
Congratulations.


00:58:26.535 --> 00:58:29.333
Why weren't you at the church?
I wanted to bomb the vicar with you.


00:58:29.415 --> 00:58:32.452
Got them off Bella's chauffeur.
I can't see anything!


00:58:34.495 --> 00:58:38.488
I'm going to have to go, Jimmy.
But light a bomb for me, won't you?


00:58:38.935 --> 00:58:40.812
-Make it a whopper.
-Mmm-hmm.


00:58:40.895 --> 00:58:42.532
Thank you, Betty.


00:58:47.014 --> 00:58:51.644
Why your mother feels the need to turn
the house into a shrubbery every Christmas,


00:58:51.734 --> 00:58:53.690
I cannot imagine.


00:59:07.774 --> 00:59:09.651
-Bye.
-Patten, remind me.


00:59:09.734 --> 00:59:13.488
Tiger Bigham has a limp,
and Tony has a monocle?


00:59:13.574 --> 00:59:15.007
-Tony has...
-Bob!


00:59:15.094 --> 00:59:17.210
Darling, how are you?


00:59:17.894 --> 00:59:21.409
Well, you haven't changed a bit.


00:59:26.054 --> 00:59:27.885
Oh, Canon Dakin.


00:59:27.974 --> 00:59:30.408
Have you met the...


00:59:30.494 --> 00:59:34.567
Well, the ritual bit is over, Professor,
but the sacrifice is only just beginning!


00:59:34.654 --> 00:59:36.133
There's one in every family.


00:59:36.214 --> 00:59:37.966
Two in yours.


00:59:38.054 --> 00:59:39.487
Thank you so much.


00:59:42.694 --> 00:59:46.208
-How old are you, Jimmy?
-Eight and a half. How old are you?


00:59:46.293 --> 00:59:47.612
I'm a little over 21.


00:59:47.693 --> 00:59:50.082
No, you're not. You're an old woman.


00:59:50.173 --> 00:59:53.643
And you, young man, are old enough
to know some manners.


00:59:53.733 --> 00:59:56.770
Didn't your mother ever tell you
it's rude to stare?


00:59:56.853 --> 00:59:57.968
No.


00:59:58.053 --> 01:00:01.329
Well, let me be the first to inform you.
It's rude to stare.


01:00:01.413 --> 01:00:03.688
I'm not staring. I'm looking.


01:00:05.413 --> 01:00:06.482
Isn't he a little lamb?


01:00:06.573 --> 01:00:08.529
Good God.


01:00:08.613 --> 01:00:10.729
Jimmy! Come here, please.


01:00:11.333 --> 01:00:14.450
All young men go through
these difficult phases, don't they?


01:00:14.533 --> 01:00:18.287
Yes, Miss Spoon, you're quite right.
No end in sight for David's, though.


01:00:20.293 --> 01:00:21.408
Mmm.


01:00:34.253 --> 01:00:35.652
It's done now.


01:00:36.933 --> 01:00:39.322
This is the fun part at last.


01:00:39.413 --> 01:00:41.449
Evie, you always have fun.


01:00:41.533 --> 01:00:44.923
It might look like fun, but this is hard work.
Trust me.


01:00:47.652 --> 01:00:51.565
You can't honestly be interested
in the Bigham twins?


01:00:51.652 --> 01:00:53.643
They're so featureless,
even they can't tell which one is which.


01:00:53.732 --> 01:00:55.131
Don't be cruel.


01:00:56.252 --> 01:00:57.810
I'm not fussy.


01:01:01.572 --> 01:01:04.086
What I want from a man is quite simple.


01:01:05.892 --> 01:01:07.120
He must be kind.


01:01:08.652 --> 01:01:10.210
And brave.


01:01:11.732 --> 01:01:14.530
Just a little bit brave. That's all.


01:01:17.852 --> 01:01:22.323
I thought I knew what she wanted,
and it wasn't any of this.


01:01:24.092 --> 01:01:26.481
She's shown you, hasn't she?


01:01:26.572 --> 01:01:29.211
She just said it out loud in a church.


01:01:32.612 --> 01:01:36.969
We all want other people
to make our decisions for us, don't we?


01:01:37.052 --> 01:01:39.850
That way we never have to blame ourselves.


01:01:45.612 --> 01:01:47.966
I'll leave you to your conquests.


01:01:55.571 --> 01:01:57.607
-What's going on?
-Oh, nothing.


01:01:58.851 --> 01:02:01.729
Only Joseph thinks he's found a wasps' nest.


01:02:01.811 --> 01:02:04.644
Tell me. The little bombs...


01:02:05.571 --> 01:02:07.562
Oh. Yes, sorry about that.


01:02:07.651 --> 01:02:11.326
No, no. They're marvellous.
How do you make them?


01:02:11.411 --> 01:02:16.565
Just a little potassium nitrate, and charcoal,
and wax rolled up with the confetti.


01:02:16.651 --> 01:02:18.050
Marvellous.


01:02:19.131 --> 01:02:22.646
Yes. Never been any good
at that sort of thing.


01:02:22.731 --> 01:02:27.486
Can't seem to offer my family
much excitement at the moment.


01:02:28.931 --> 01:02:31.923
Can't be easy, being a good father.


01:02:33.171 --> 01:02:35.605
Not half as hard as being a bad one.


01:02:35.691 --> 01:02:38.205
Jimmy, will you come here this instant?


01:02:38.291 --> 01:02:40.009
What do you think of Joseph?


01:02:40.091 --> 01:02:42.127
Well, he seems an amusing young man.


01:02:42.211 --> 01:02:44.566
That's it, isn't it? He's amusing.


01:02:45.371 --> 01:02:47.805
Young women like amusing men, don't they?


01:02:47.891 --> 01:02:50.245
I suppose they think it's dangerous.


01:02:50.330 --> 01:02:52.890
Dolly's always been attracted to danger.


01:02:52.970 --> 01:02:54.119
Aren't we all?


01:02:54.210 --> 01:02:57.247
Of course, she doesn't know the difference
between amusing and dangerous!


01:02:57.330 --> 01:03:00.845
-Unlike you, Nancy.
-Really?


01:03:00.930 --> 01:03:04.400
Well, you're so lucky to have David
who is so dependable.


01:03:05.130 --> 01:03:09.043
And amusing sometimes, too!


01:03:09.130 --> 01:03:11.803
Don't you think these young people
need saving from themselves?


01:03:13.530 --> 01:03:15.327
Did you hear that one, Uncle Horace?


01:03:15.410 --> 01:03:17.321
Yes. Yes, I got that one.


01:03:17.410 --> 01:03:18.399
That's enough, Jimmy!


01:03:18.490 --> 01:03:20.003
Who gave that boy those wretched things?


01:03:20.090 --> 01:03:21.409
Joseph made them specially.


01:03:21.490 --> 01:03:23.242
I should have known.


01:03:23.330 --> 01:03:27.926
My God, if you want one good reason
not to marry, go to a family wedding.


01:03:29.290 --> 01:03:30.564
Come on, you.


01:03:30.930 --> 01:03:32.329
There you are!


01:03:33.450 --> 01:03:35.441
Are chauffeurs allowed to drink?


01:03:35.530 --> 01:03:39.318
Surely no one could object
to a glass of champagne at Christmas?


01:03:40.130 --> 01:03:42.439
He must be awfully cold out there.


01:03:44.570 --> 01:03:47.721
-Anyway, it was lovely, wasn't it?
-What was?


01:03:47.810 --> 01:03:50.404
Oh, don't be so cynical, Mr Anthropologist.


01:03:50.490 --> 01:03:54.528
Surely you must have appreciated the event
from an academic perspective.


01:03:54.609 --> 01:03:57.806
Marriage rituals,
and the herd instinct, and all that.


01:03:57.889 --> 01:04:02.167
-Anyway, I hope you didn't get stung.
-What?


01:04:02.249 --> 01:04:03.682
By the wasps.


01:04:06.209 --> 01:04:09.360
If I were Dolly and Owen, I wouldn't want
to spend another second in this house!


01:04:09.449 --> 01:04:13.044
I'd be halfway to Argentina with nothing
but the starlight and the sunset for company!


01:04:13.129 --> 01:04:15.120
Don't be such an ass.


01:04:15.209 --> 01:04:17.040
-Time to pose.
-What? Now?


01:04:18.489 --> 01:04:21.083
You said Tony had a limp,
and Tiger had a moustache!


01:04:21.169 --> 01:04:22.204
I meant a lisp.


01:04:22.289 --> 01:04:24.564
Well, they're both clean-shaven, limp or lisp!


01:04:24.649 --> 01:04:28.119
Kitty, outside, please.
The photographer's waiting.


01:04:28.649 --> 01:04:29.684
Ugh!


01:04:30.889 --> 01:04:33.119
I shouldn't hang about
for Dolly's sake, Joseph.


01:04:33.209 --> 01:04:34.961
Why not get Millman to order you a taxi?


01:04:35.049 --> 01:04:37.165
She's ordered one already for me,
Mrs Thatcham.


01:04:37.249 --> 01:04:40.127
But I can't leave
without toasting the happy couple.


01:04:48.329 --> 01:04:50.718
Kitty! The photographs, now!


01:04:51.489 --> 01:04:53.081
Have a seat, Tom.


01:04:55.728 --> 01:04:58.959
Now remember, this is the happiest day
of your lives!


01:05:42.368 --> 01:05:44.006
One more, please.


01:05:46.408 --> 01:05:47.921
Tom! Tom! Tom!


01:05:52.408 --> 01:05:55.400
Did you see Tom? As drunk as a skunk.


01:05:55.488 --> 01:05:57.125
I can't think where he finds it.


01:05:57.207 --> 01:05:59.198
Well, when the groom comes
looking for the ring,


01:05:59.287 --> 01:06:02.962
it's hardly likely to be a conventional
wedding now, is it?


01:06:03.047 --> 01:06:05.845
I hope that Bigham boy knows
what he's taking on.


01:06:05.927 --> 01:06:07.679
Oh, I'm sure he knows.


01:06:08.327 --> 01:06:10.079
Strange creature.


01:06:11.967 --> 01:06:13.559
Yes, the pipes!


01:06:16.327 --> 01:06:19.524
Can you hear this, Uncle Horace?


01:06:30.807 --> 01:06:32.035
Hello?


01:06:34.967 --> 01:06:36.480
Kitty!


01:06:36.567 --> 01:06:37.761
Kitty!


01:06:38.927 --> 01:06:41.202
Could you give Dolly a message for me
before she leaves?


01:06:41.287 --> 01:06:44.245
So Evie gets both Bigham twins,
and I get to run messages for you?


01:06:44.327 --> 01:06:47.239
Come on.
Just say, "Joseph sends his love." That's all.


01:06:47.327 --> 01:06:50.558
-Why can't you tell her yourself?
-She doesn't want to see me.


01:06:50.647 --> 01:06:54.083
-Please.
-All right.


01:06:54.167 --> 01:06:57.045
-But you have to do something for me.
-And what's that?


01:06:57.127 --> 01:06:59.276
-Tea in London.
-My pleasure.


01:06:59.366 --> 01:07:01.675
And you have to bring along
some eligible boys for me.


01:07:01.766 --> 01:07:03.836
Kitty, you're far too good for eligible boys.


01:07:03.926 --> 01:07:05.723
What you need are some ineligible ones.


01:07:08.166 --> 01:07:09.485
All right.


01:07:10.926 --> 01:07:13.440
-Whatever you've got.
-Done.


01:07:14.606 --> 01:07:16.756
But not that one
that looks like he's got rabies.


01:07:16.846 --> 01:07:17.961
Go!


01:07:45.526 --> 01:07:46.641
Hello!


01:07:50.246 --> 01:07:52.396
Ladies and gentlemen,


01:07:52.486 --> 01:07:55.876
Owen and Dolly certainly appear
to be the perfect couple.


01:07:55.966 --> 01:07:59.925
But we hope Dolly is prepared to share
with Owen his other interests.


01:08:00.005 --> 01:08:02.473
When he was 12, he loved rugby.


01:08:02.565 --> 01:08:04.874
When he was 15, he loved rugby.


01:08:04.965 --> 01:08:07.274
When he was 21, he loved rugby.


01:08:07.365 --> 01:08:09.833
Now, he loves rugby and Dolly.


01:08:09.925 --> 01:08:13.634
In actual fact,
they complement each other very well.


01:08:13.725 --> 01:08:16.717
-They're a handsome couple.
-Aren't they?


01:08:18.365 --> 01:08:21.721
-Owen seems a good fellow.
-He is.


01:08:24.165 --> 01:08:26.395
I can't say I saw it coming.


01:08:27.245 --> 01:08:29.361
Mind you, I'm a dreadful judge of character.


01:08:29.445 --> 01:08:32.403
-I don't believe that.
-It's quite true.


01:08:33.245 --> 01:08:35.156
My wife always said so.


01:08:37.805 --> 01:08:41.275
Female intuition is no longer a feature
of my life, you see.


01:08:41.365 --> 01:08:43.037
I'm lost without it.


01:08:44.325 --> 01:08:47.874
Well, you know what to do
if something's missing, don't you?


01:08:49.885 --> 01:08:53.241
-What?
-Pray to St Anthony.


01:08:54.485 --> 01:08:55.838
To Argentina!


01:08:55.925 --> 01:08:57.995
-To Argentina!
-To Argentina!


01:08:58.085 --> 01:08:59.279
-To Argentina!
-To Argentina!


01:08:59.365 --> 01:09:01.162
And to Albania.


01:10:30.283 --> 01:10:32.558
Jimmy, put those away at once.


01:10:32.643 --> 01:10:34.520
I've only got four left.


01:10:34.603 --> 01:10:36.878
-I'll make some more if you like.
-No, you can't, Daddy.


01:10:36.963 --> 01:10:40.717
Yes, just a little potassium sulphide,


01:10:40.803 --> 01:10:43.442
charcoal wrapped up with the old confetti.


01:10:43.523 --> 01:10:47.152
Daddy wants me to light one near Aunt Bella
to see if her breath catches fire.


01:10:47.243 --> 01:10:49.518
Does he? Very amusing of him.


01:10:53.283 --> 01:10:55.877
Nancy, have you seen Joseph?


01:10:55.963 --> 01:10:58.557
I think you'll find him in the library.


01:11:10.642 --> 01:11:12.997
Why does Dolly look so cross?


01:11:14.002 --> 01:11:16.072
It's been a long day.


01:11:18.282 --> 01:11:21.991
Tom, could you ask Millman
for another jam tart for Jimmy?


01:11:22.082 --> 01:11:24.312
Just a second.
The groom is looking for his bride.


01:11:24.402 --> 01:11:25.755
Now, Tom.


01:11:27.602 --> 01:11:29.638
Right away, Mrs Dakin.


01:11:35.802 --> 01:11:38.600
It's a shame
your mother couldn't come down today.


01:11:38.682 --> 01:11:40.752
Is she all right?


01:11:40.842 --> 01:11:41.991
She's fine.


01:11:43.442 --> 01:11:47.674
-You'll say I asked after her, won't you?
-Yes.


01:11:49.962 --> 01:11:52.795
You're doing a new course
of lectures, aren't you?


01:11:52.882 --> 01:11:55.112
-Yes.
-Six months, isn't it?


01:11:55.202 --> 01:11:56.760
Yes.


01:11:56.842 --> 01:11:58.878
Do you know what you'll be doing after that?


01:11:58.962 --> 01:11:59.951
No.


01:12:00.042 --> 01:12:02.476
-Will you be going away?
-I might.


01:12:04.442 --> 01:12:05.716
No, I...


01:12:06.362 --> 01:12:07.954
Oh, for God's sake,
can we stop talking like this?


01:12:08.042 --> 01:12:11.192
No, really. I want to know about it.
I'm interested.


01:12:21.321 --> 01:12:22.720
Joseph.


01:12:24.401 --> 01:12:26.073
Dearest Joseph.


01:12:27.521 --> 01:12:31.594
Come and sit down.
What is it all about? Really, you must tell me.


01:12:34.881 --> 01:12:38.476
It's no good.
It's no use asking me what the matter is.


01:12:38.561 --> 01:12:39.994
I don't know myself.


01:12:47.841 --> 01:12:51.231
Evelyn said some time ago, not to me,


01:12:52.641 --> 01:12:56.156
-that she thought you were in love with me.
-Did she?


01:12:58.001 --> 01:13:01.118
Well, perhaps I was some time ago.
But I'm not now.


01:13:01.201 --> 01:13:02.793
I haven't been for a good while.


01:13:02.881 --> 01:13:05.793
Why do you never tell anybody anything?


01:13:05.881 --> 01:13:09.476
-I thought we understood each other.
-Oh, I do understand. I do.


01:13:09.561 --> 01:13:14.111
You always like to make out
like you are on top of the wave, oblivious,


01:13:14.200 --> 01:13:16.270
that you can never be in need
of anyone else's help.


01:13:16.360 --> 01:13:18.316
No, that's not me.


01:13:18.400 --> 01:13:19.992
What is it that you think
I should have said, Joseph?


01:13:20.080 --> 01:13:21.069
That night in Malton...


01:13:21.160 --> 01:13:23.549
That night in Malton, you had three ciders
and half a bottle of gin.


01:13:23.640 --> 01:13:27.269
Oh, for once, can we just say
what it is we're talking about?


01:13:27.360 --> 01:13:29.749
Why didn't you tell me
that you were going to marry Owen?


01:13:29.840 --> 01:13:31.239
I wrote, and I told you about it.


01:13:31.320 --> 01:13:34.039
Oh, yes. That letter from Albania.


01:13:35.360 --> 01:13:37.828
Why so soon? Barely two months.


01:13:37.920 --> 01:13:41.674
I only decided two months ago.
And he asked, and I wanted to say yes.


01:13:41.760 --> 01:13:44.593
What if you had come to Greece,
not Albania?


01:13:46.400 --> 01:13:47.435
Hmm?


01:13:47.600 --> 01:13:49.955
What if your mother hadn't posted you off
to the honourable Owen?


01:13:50.600 --> 01:13:53.194
I've never been made to do anything.


01:13:54.240 --> 01:13:57.550
What if I had stopped the wedding, Dolly?
What would you have done then?


01:13:57.640 --> 01:14:00.791
What if this? What if that!
What if you weren't you, Joseph?


01:14:02.880 --> 01:14:06.953
Anyway, why all the fuss? For God's sake.


01:14:10.080 --> 01:14:12.150
You don't want to marry me.


01:14:16.319 --> 01:14:20.028
No. No, I know that.


01:14:34.919 --> 01:14:36.238
Dolly!


01:14:48.319 --> 01:14:49.911
Sorry.


01:14:51.319 --> 01:14:52.308
Um...


01:14:53.119 --> 01:14:55.587
It's time to go, Dolly, darling.


01:14:55.679 --> 01:15:00.275
Everybody is standing out on the doorstep
waiting to bid us a fond farewell.


01:15:03.239 --> 01:15:04.797
I'm sorry to interrupt, darling,


01:15:04.879 --> 01:15:08.235
but what is all this about taking
a tortoise along or something or other?


01:15:08.319 --> 01:15:10.514
Onboard the liner with us?


01:15:10.599 --> 01:15:13.397
Millman said there was a tortoise
waiting for me in the kitchen.


01:15:13.479 --> 01:15:15.787
Well, that's all right. It's my tortoise.


01:15:15.878 --> 01:15:20.076
Well, it may be your tortoise, Dolly,
but what is it going to eat crossing over


01:15:20.158 --> 01:15:22.513
on the boat, is what I'm wondering.


01:15:22.598 --> 01:15:26.511
For God's sake! Do come in, Owen!
What's the matter with you?


01:15:26.598 --> 01:15:30.034
Surely there must be something
on a big boat like that for a tortoise to eat.


01:15:30.118 --> 01:15:31.107
I mean, really!


01:15:31.198 --> 01:15:34.315
That's all very well, but I'm afraid not,
Dolly, all the same.


01:15:34.398 --> 01:15:36.992
Can't it eat dried
peas or something?


01:15:37.078 --> 01:15:38.431
I don't think a tortoise...


01:15:38.518 --> 01:15:40.986
Owen, please stop hovering like that.


01:15:41.078 --> 01:15:42.431
It...


01:15:42.518 --> 01:15:46.306
I mean she likes to eat water biscuits.
I know that for a fact.


01:15:46.398 --> 01:15:48.514
And these big liners are loaded with them.


01:15:48.598 --> 01:15:50.475
Well, that may be so,
but if you will forgive me for doing so,


01:15:50.558 --> 01:15:53.391
I'm afraid I've told Millman
we are not going to take the tortoise.


01:15:53.478 --> 01:15:54.467
You did what?


01:15:54.558 --> 01:15:56.833
It's vanished anyway. No one can find it.


01:15:56.918 --> 01:15:58.033
My God, this is outrageous!


01:15:58.118 --> 01:16:00.393
It's probably half-way across the fields
to Malton by now,


01:16:00.478 --> 01:16:02.594
where, believe me,
it will be much happier than...


01:16:02.678 --> 01:16:03.667
There you are!


01:16:03.798 --> 01:16:06.073
We couldn't think
where on Earth you had got to.


01:16:06.158 --> 01:16:08.592
What are you doing in here?


01:16:09.038 --> 01:16:10.232
Joseph?


01:16:11.758 --> 01:16:14.795
Well, thank you for finding Dolly for us.


01:16:14.878 --> 01:16:17.437
Come along, dear. Everybody's waiting.


01:16:20.557 --> 01:16:22.229
For goodness sake!


01:16:26.037 --> 01:16:29.109
You don't know when
you're beaten, do you?


01:16:30.357 --> 01:16:32.507
Why can't you just leave us alone?


01:16:50.957 --> 01:16:52.276
Hello?


01:16:54.277 --> 01:16:57.030
I can't seem to open the door.


01:17:01.717 --> 01:17:02.866
Hello?


01:17:08.797 --> 01:17:10.435
-Goodbye.
-Goodbye.


01:17:11.837 --> 01:17:13.509
Good luck, Doll!


01:17:22.596 --> 01:17:23.915
You will write, won't you?


01:17:29.916 --> 01:17:31.429
Goodbye!


01:17:44.636 --> 01:17:47.514
No limp, no lisp, no moustache, no idea.


01:17:47.596 --> 01:17:50.394
I must say, the tattoo's a complete mystery.


01:17:50.476 --> 01:17:52.034
What did I tell you?


01:17:52.116 --> 01:17:54.186
Two men from school present
at the ceremony.


01:17:54.276 --> 01:17:56.187
That gangly one was all bussing up to me.


01:17:56.276 --> 01:17:59.905
I hope Owen knows his way
around these ghastly little roads.


01:17:59.996 --> 01:18:03.750
They might never make it to Torbay,
never mind Buenos Aires!


01:18:03.836 --> 01:18:05.792
I should think they're thrilled
to be alone at last.


01:18:05.876 --> 01:18:09.551
Well, if you want one good reason
not to get married, go to a family wedding.


01:18:10.716 --> 01:18:13.435
So sorry. Champagne up the nose.


01:18:16.716 --> 01:18:18.274
Are you all right?


01:18:20.875 --> 01:18:24.709
Why not go upstairs
and have a good lie-down, Joseph? I would.


01:18:24.795 --> 01:18:27.753
No, thank you.
My taxi will be here in a minute.


01:18:27.835 --> 01:18:30.349
Well, why not go up
even if only for a minute?


01:18:30.435 --> 01:18:32.551
You're not doing yourself
or anybody else any good


01:18:32.635 --> 01:18:35.024
by moping round in here
with a face like that, you know.


01:18:35.115 --> 01:18:37.913
Run upstairs. Everyone else is headed home.


01:18:38.595 --> 01:18:41.189
If I were you, I'd need a good lie-down.


01:18:41.275 --> 01:18:42.788
Hettie!


01:18:43.875 --> 01:18:47.504
I do apologise for appearing
at the drawing room dressed like this,


01:18:47.595 --> 01:18:49.347
but a rather strange thing has happened.


01:18:49.435 --> 01:18:52.552
I ran up to my bedroom
in order to take a quick bath.


01:18:52.635 --> 01:18:55.354
When I returned from the
bathroom, I found...


01:18:59.115 --> 01:19:01.993
Ladies underclothes, apparently,
scattered around the room.


01:19:05.035 --> 01:19:08.345
Someone has evidently mistaken
my bedroom for her own.


01:19:08.435 --> 01:19:09.629
Just gets better and better.


01:19:09.715 --> 01:19:11.148
How extraordinary.


01:19:11.235 --> 01:19:14.864
Now, Miss Spoon,
what on Earth can I do for you?


01:19:15.555 --> 01:19:18.069
Mrs Thatcham,
it really is most awfully embarrassing.


01:19:18.155 --> 01:19:20.669
I went to have a bath and came back
to my room for a moment


01:19:20.755 --> 01:19:23.348
and all my things had been...


01:19:23.434 --> 01:19:24.708
Dear God!


01:19:26.994 --> 01:19:29.349
-Unpacked.
-What room are you in?


01:19:29.434 --> 01:19:31.743
The Chinese room,
where you yourself put me.


01:19:31.834 --> 01:19:32.949
Mum!


01:19:33.034 --> 01:19:36.583
Why wasn't I put in the Chinese room?
That's where the action is!


01:19:36.674 --> 01:19:37.948
Oh, for goodness...


01:19:38.034 --> 01:19:40.389
I knew this would happen.


01:19:40.474 --> 01:19:43.193
Don't pretend this has never happened
to you before, Bob!


01:19:43.274 --> 01:19:45.310
Not as often as I would like.


01:19:45.394 --> 01:19:47.191
Well?


01:19:48.474 --> 01:19:51.625
Poor Miss Spoon.
Perhaps she is over-tired.


01:19:51.714 --> 01:19:55.024
Do take a seat, Hettie.
You must be worn out.


01:19:55.114 --> 01:19:57.150
I'm fine, Bob dear.


01:20:00.754 --> 01:20:01.982
Oh, dear!


01:20:03.274 --> 01:20:06.710
Some young men never think
of anybody but themselves.


01:20:10.354 --> 01:20:12.584
To me that seems so curious!


01:20:16.434 --> 01:20:19.904
Have a chocolate, Robert.
Kitty? So very delicious.


01:20:22.154 --> 01:20:23.269
Thank you.


01:20:25.113 --> 01:20:28.185
Joseph, why don't you just help yourself?


01:20:30.513 --> 01:20:36.429
You know, you young men nowadays
always seem to mope about the place.


01:20:37.353 --> 01:20:40.550
Never seeming to care
to pull yourselves together,


01:20:40.633 --> 01:20:43.989
stand up nice and square
and walk along properly.


01:20:45.793 --> 01:20:48.182
Or to join in with other people's fun.


01:20:49.513 --> 01:20:51.265
Don't you know what I mean?


01:20:51.353 --> 01:20:53.787
And yet you're never lagging behind,
I notice,


01:20:53.873 --> 01:20:58.105
when it comes to raising your voices
in criticism of other people.


01:20:58.193 --> 01:20:59.546
-Now, you, Joseph...
-Mum...


01:20:59.633 --> 01:21:02.466
You, Joseph, have had everything
you want from life.


01:21:02.553 --> 01:21:04.987
The profession you wanted,
a first-rate education,


01:21:05.073 --> 01:21:09.351
a most devoted mother, and yet here
you seem to be all up against everybody.


01:21:09.433 --> 01:21:11.788
Nothing seems good enough for you.


01:21:13.673 --> 01:21:16.346
Now I may be very dense, I freely confess,


01:21:16.433 --> 01:21:19.743
but I utterly fail to understand it!


01:21:25.393 --> 01:21:28.623
Of course, you may be very dense.


01:21:28.712 --> 01:21:30.862
That might be the very reason.


01:21:32.032 --> 01:21:35.502
You can't understand why no one arrives
when you think they should.


01:21:35.592 --> 01:21:36.707
And then you can't understand


01:21:36.792 --> 01:21:39.989
why the canon and Miss Spoon come
to be sharing a bedroom, can you?


01:21:40.072 --> 01:21:42.905
You couldn't grasp how it was that
Millman could have been such an odd person


01:21:42.992 --> 01:21:44.391
as to lay the luncheon in the dining room!


01:21:44.472 --> 01:21:48.226
In fact, you don't really understand anyone
or anything around you, do you?


01:21:48.312 --> 01:21:50.667
I'm sure I don't know
what on Earth you mean.


01:21:50.752 --> 01:21:53.585
I ought not to tell you this,
I suppose, but I'm going to.


01:21:53.672 --> 01:21:57.631
You don't know, do you, why your daughter
married with such indecent haste?


01:21:57.712 --> 01:22:00.545
Of course in another month or two,
we would all know why.


01:22:00.632 --> 01:22:03.942
Mind you, as to whom the father is,
well, there I am as ignorant as you are.


01:22:04.032 --> 01:22:05.147
You're mad.


01:22:05.232 --> 01:22:07.427
It was inevitable that you would be
the last person to know about it.


01:22:07.512 --> 01:22:09.184
Stop it. Think about what you're saying.


01:22:09.272 --> 01:22:11.308
Yes, it may be mine.


01:22:11.392 --> 01:22:14.862
They're having a sweepstake in the kitchen.
It's not too late to place a bet.


01:22:14.952 --> 01:22:17.512
-Joseph!
-My money's on Aunt Bella's chauffeur.


01:22:17.592 --> 01:22:20.504
Three-to-one on Owen
and 100-to-1 on the tortoise!


01:22:20.592 --> 01:22:23.868
Of course, Dolly must have despaired
at even attempting to explain it to you.


01:22:23.952 --> 01:22:27.786
-Explain what?
-That this isn't the first time either!


01:22:27.872 --> 01:22:30.465
Why else do you think
Dolly Thatcham is always running away?


01:22:30.551 --> 01:22:35.386
I have no idea what on Earth
you are talking about!


01:22:35.471 --> 01:22:38.508
What I am talking about is perfectly simple.


01:22:42.191 --> 01:22:45.786
Dolly has broken my heart, and I let her.


01:22:50.071 --> 01:22:53.427
Thank you all for a most delightful day.


01:23:04.711 --> 01:23:05.939
Good God!


01:23:06.471 --> 01:23:08.348
Heavens! Are you all right?


01:23:09.311 --> 01:23:11.029
Well, I heard all that well enough.


01:23:11.111 --> 01:23:13.147
Why did he say those things?
You don't think...


01:23:13.231 --> 01:23:14.983
Of course not, Mama.


01:23:15.071 --> 01:23:18.746
Don't pay any attention. He's drunk.
That's what's the matter with him.


01:23:18.831 --> 01:23:21.391
Let's get you tidied up and away
from all these grown-ups.


01:23:21.471 --> 01:23:23.143
Sorry, Aunt Bella.


01:23:23.231 --> 01:23:25.347
He's missed the boat.


01:23:25.431 --> 01:23:26.989
Let him go now, Hettie.


01:23:34.510 --> 01:23:38.219
-Now are you glad you came?
-I couldn't have asked for more.


01:23:49.710 --> 01:23:51.029
David!


01:23:54.470 --> 01:23:57.542
Yes, so dreadfully disappointing
you were not able to get over.


01:23:57.630 --> 01:23:59.780
Your lampshade was
very much appreciated.


01:23:59.870 --> 01:24:02.589
Dolly's had it packed for South America.


01:24:03.270 --> 01:24:06.580
Imagine that, Dodo!
Everybody admired it. Everybody!


01:24:06.670 --> 01:24:10.709
So wonderfully clever of you.
Thank you so very much, dear.


01:24:11.510 --> 01:24:14.707
Oh, they were there.
Yes, I saw them in the back.


01:24:17.430 --> 01:24:18.943
Yes, it was.


01:24:44.149 --> 01:24:47.664
Mrs Whitstable was most insistent
you should take this with you, sir.


01:24:47.749 --> 01:24:49.626
Most insistent, she was.


01:24:56.549 --> 01:24:58.028
Thank you, Millman.


01:25:03.549 --> 01:25:06.859
Of course,
we had such cheerful weather.

